WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:06.552
"Do Vitamin D Supplements Help Prevent Diabetes, Cancer Mortality, and Overall Mortality?"

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:08.940
The VITAL study
was a randomized trial

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:10.740
to test whether
vitamin D supplementation

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:12.600
could prevent
cardiovascular disease

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:13.140
or cancer.

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:16.320
It also tested fish oil
and convincingly showed

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:18.360
that the use of omega-3s
is not effective

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:19.980
in preventing
cardiovascular disease,

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:22.260
but normal-weight
study participants

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:24.300
randomized to vitamin D
did end up

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:26.040
having a lower incidence
of cancer.

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:27.540
Over a period of five years,

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:30.240
a 24 percent lower risk
of getting cancer.

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:33.420
But overweight
or obese individuals did not.

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:36.300
The dose used in the study,
2,000 units a day,

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:38.040
may not have led
to similar results

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:39.660
in overweight
and obese participants

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:41.760
due to the higher
vitamin D requirements

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:42.660
of such individuals.

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:45.840
2,000 units a day may be enough
to get normal weight people

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:48.540
up to target,
but obese adults may need

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:49.920
at least
two to three times more,

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:52.560
a dose closer to 3,000
to 6,000 units a day

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:54.180
to reach the same blood levels.

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:56.760
A similar result was found

00:00:56.760 --> 00:00:59.220
in the largest ever
vitamin D diabetes trial,

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:02.220
where pre-diabetics randomized
to take 4,000 units a day

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:04.920
reduced their risk of slipping
into full-blown diabetes

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:06.570
by 29 percent,

00:01:06.570 --> 00:01:10.020
but it seemed to work
only if they were not obese.

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:12.420
That's why,
in the future perhaps,

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:14.340
vitamin D clinical trials
should be based on

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:16.620
achieved vitamin D
concentrations in the blood,

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:19.500
rather than
one-size-fits-all dosing.

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:21.720
For example, if you look at
vitamin D concentrations

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:23.640
achieved in
breast cancer trials,

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:26.700
regardless of which group
the subjects were randomized to,

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:29.460
there was a whopping
82 percent lower incidence rate

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:31.800
of breast cancer for women
with vitamin D concentrations

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:33.840
greater than or equal to
60 ng/ml

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:35.640
versus less than 20,

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:37.200
and the higher
their levels were,

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:39.120
the lower
their breast cancer risk

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:39.840
seemed to fall.

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:42.060
But if you just
lump everyone together,

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:44.280
it's no longer
a randomized controlled trial,

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:46.200
and we get back to the issue
of confounding,

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:48.900
like maybe the women
only had higher levels

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:49.980
of the sunshine vitamin

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:51.960
because they were outside
jogging every day,

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:53.580
and we know that
physical activity alone

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:55.920
may decrease breast cancer risk.

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:58.620
The finding of benefit
in certain subgroups

00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:00.660
of study individuals,
like less cancer

00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:01.980
among those
who are not overweight,

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:04.380
or less diabetes
for those who are not obese,

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:07.380
are examples
of secondary analyses,

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:09.420
where you go back
after the trial's over

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:11.460
to see whether
it may have worked

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:13.560
in some subset,
even if the intervention

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:15.000
had flopped overall.

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:16.980
This isn't uncommon,

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:20.460
but such results
must be interpreted cautiously,

00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:23.700
because you can slice and dice
practically any data set

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:26.220
and come up with
some sort of spurious fluke

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:27.420
if you try hard enough.

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:30.360
But if you put all of
the randomized controlled trials

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:32.100
on vitamin D supplementation
for the prevention

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:33.540
of type 2 diabetes together,

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:37.140
daily supplementation with 1,000
or more units of vitamin D

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:40.560
does indeed reduce the risk
of diabetes among pre-diabetics.

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:43.800
In the 3 large trials
that were specifically designed

00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:45.780
and conducted
for the prevention of diabetes,

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:48.060
vitamin D supplementation
reduced the risk

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:50.940
of developing diabetes
by about 10 percent overall.

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:53.520
What about putting all
the randomized controlled

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:55.860
vitamin D studies together
for cancer prevention?

00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:58.440
Those randomized
to take vitamin D supplements

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:00.720
were 13 percent less likely
to die from cancer

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:02.820
over the subsequent
3 to 10 years.

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:05.580
Now vitamin D supplementation
wasn't able

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:07.380
to reduce the risk
of getting cancer,

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:09.360
but it was shown
to significantly reduce

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:11.100
the risk of dying from cancer.

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:12.660
I mean, it's a tiny benefit,

00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:15.720
basically taking these people's
chances of dying from cancer

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:19.440
over a span of a few years
from around 24 in 1,000

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:21.360
to, like, 21 in 1,000,

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:23.280
but it's a beneficial effect

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:25.380
on lowering cancer mortality
nonetheless,

00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:27.600
based on
a comprehensive analysis

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:29.400
of 10 randomized
controlled trials

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:31.620
involving more than
80,000 participants.

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:35.040
Note that a 13 percent reduction
in cancer mortality

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:38.160
doesn't necessarily mean
you live any longer.

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:41.160
Since vitamin D supplements
don't help heart disease,

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:43.200
killer number one,
you would only expect

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:45.060
your lifespan
to significantly benefit

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:47.580
if your heart disease risk
is really low.

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:49.560
Only if you're protected
from heart disease

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:52.320
would killer number two
take on a more prominent role,

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:53.520
or if there was
some other reason

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:55.380
you were at
particularly high cancer risk.

00:03:56.100 --> 00:03:58.380
For those getting
inadequate sunshine,

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:00.840
I recommend
2,000 international units

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:02.160
a day of vitamin D3.

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:04.440
Daily dosing is important

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:06.840
since taking monthly
or yearly mega-doses

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:08.940
doesn't seem to work
for reducing cancer mortality.

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:11.760
And D3 may be preferable.

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:13.200
Historically,
it's been suggested

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:15.240
there's no difference
in terms of effectiveness

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:16.620
between the two types
you can buy,

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:18.000
vitamins D2 and D3,

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:20.940
but it turns out that vitamin D3
may work better,

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:24.660
as it can increase D levels
by about 75 percent,

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:26.280
compared to the same dose of D2,

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:28.320
only increasing levels
by about a third.

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:31.380
So it looks like D3
is about twice as effective

00:04:31.380 --> 00:04:32.700
at raising blood levels.

00:04:33.420 --> 00:04:36.060
Does it matter if you get
powder-filled capsules,

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:37.260
oil-based preparations,

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:39.180
or water-based preparations
of vitamin D?

00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:42.840
Nope, they all appear
to raise your D levels the same.

